course: religion-ethics
lesson: 94
title: Why cheating hurts the cheat
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Cheating can look like a shortcut, but it usually hurts other people and, in the end, the cheat as well: they lose learning, pride, trust and sometimes their good name.
explain: Daisy ran fair and came second; the girl who won cut a corner of the cross-country course, so she has the prize, while Daisy has her honest run and nothing to hide.
explain: At the ancient Olympic Games, cheats paid fines that built bronze statues of Zeus with their names carved on the base, so everyone walking into the stadium remembered them as cheats.
explain: Cheating "the system", like a fake address or fake votes, is never victimless: there is always a real person who loses a place, a vote or a fair chance.
explain: Many faiths agree: Christians and Jews read "Do not steal. Do not lie. Do not deceive one another" (Leviticus 19:11), and Muslims remember the Prophet Muhammad's words, "Whoever cheats is not one of us."
fact: "Do not fret because of those who are evil" (Psalm 37:1-2); Psalm 73 is a whole prayer about cheats who seem to win.
fact: Plato argued that being truly fair is better for your soul than only seeming fair.
fact: At ancient Olympia, cheats' fines paid for bronze statues of Zeus, called Zanes, with the cheat's name on the base; "The name of the wicked will rot" (Proverbs 10:7).
fact: School places are shared out by rules; a fake address takes the place of another real child; "Give to everyone what you owe them" (Romans 13:7).
fact: Votes only mean something if each person gets one: one person, one vote.
fact: "Do not help a guilty person by being a malicious witness. Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:1-2).
fact: A fisherman's tale everyone knows will grow is a joke; claiming a fake record in a competition is cheating.
fact: Lying about how you got something, like a game skin, is empty pride; "Dishonest money dwindles away, but whoever gathers money little by little makes it grow" (Proverbs 13:11).
fact: Paying someone to write your essay steals credit and lies to the teacher: "You shall not steal" (Exodus 20:15); universities treat it as serious cheating.
fact: With a friend who cheats in a small test, talk to them first (Matthew 18:15); it's your judgement whether to tell, but don't lie for them if asked.
fact: "All hard work brings a profit" (Proverbs 14:23): making a cheat sheet carefully can teach you everything on it.
fact: The Prophet Muhammad found wet grain hidden under dry grain at a market stall and said, "Whoever cheats is not one of us" (Sahih Muslim).
fact: Faiths agree that cheating is stealing and lying at the same time.
fact: Slang check: "wicked" (Maine, New England) means very; "y'all" (US South) means you all.
exercise: Daisy came second fairly; the winner cut a corner. Is honesty worth it if cheats win? | Yes; Daisy keeps her honest run with nothing to hide, and she can tell the organiser what she saw | No; next time Daisy should cut the corner too | No; prizes are all that matter in a race | Yes, but only if the cheat gets caught later
exercise: What did the ancient Greeks do with cheats' fines at Olympia? | Built bronze statues of Zeus with the cheats' names on them | Gave the money to the winners as extra prizes for running fairly | Threw the money into the sea | Built a new running track
exercise: Why did the Greeks carve cheats' names on the statues? | Shame was part of the punishment, so everyone remembered | To thank the cheats for paying for such beautiful bronze statues | Because cheats were famous athletes | So that cheats could visit their statues
exercise: Ellie's aunt might give a fake address to get her son into a better school. Who loses? | A real child who should have had that place | Nobody; schools have endless places | Only the school, which gets one extra child | Only the aunt, if she is caught
exercise: Josh's friend wants him to vote ten times using different accounts. Is that cheating? | Yes; one person, one vote | No; it's only a class poll | No; it's helping a friend win | Only if he votes more than twenty times
exercise: Uncle Pete says the fish was "wicked small, just say it was huge". What does "wicked" mean here? | Very | Evil | Not | Quite
exercise: When is a fishing boast a real problem? | When it's entered as a fake record in a competition | When it's told as a joke that everyone at the table gets | When it's about a very small fish | When it's said in front of children
exercise: Kai bought a rare game skin but says he unlocked it by skill. What is honest? | Say how he really got it | Keep the story, it's only a game | Buy another to make it true | Say his cousin unlocked it for him
exercise: Why is paying for an essay a big deal? | It cheats the teacher, other students and the buyer, who learns nothing | Because essays are very expensive to buy | Because the website might write a bad essay | It isn't, if the essay is really good
exercise: Freya sees her best mate looking at answers in a times-tables test. What is a good first step? | Talk to her friend first, and never lie for him if asked | Shout "cheat" across the classroom | Copy the same answers so they match | Tell everyone at lunch what happened, so the whole class knows
exercise: Brayden's cheat sheet took so long to make that he learned it all. What should he do? | Not use it secretly; the learning is already his | Use it anyway, because he worked so hard on making it | Make a second, even smaller one that's easier to hide | Sell it to his friends, since it's too good to waste
exercise: What did the Prophet Muhammad say after finding wet grain hidden under dry? | Whoever cheats is not one of us | Sell the wet grain at half price | Grain sellers can never be trusted | Keep the dry grain for yourself
exercise: What do many faiths agree cheating is? | Stealing and lying at the same time | A small mistake that does not matter | Only wrong if you are caught | Fine if the other person is rich
exercise: What does Proverbs 10:7 say? | The name of the wicked will rot | The name of the wicked will shine | Names do not matter to God | The wicked will never be remembered
exercise: Which psalm is a whole prayer about cheats seeming to win? | Psalm 73 | Psalm 23 | Psalm 100 | Psalm 1
test: Your mate says, "Cheating's only bad for the people you beat." What is missing? | It hurts the cheat too: no learning, no pride, and lost trust | Nothing; cheats always come out on top, and everyone knows it | It's only bad if a teacher sees | It's good for everyone in the end
test: "That girl cut the corner and got the medal, so cheating works, innit," says your cousin. What is the best reply? | She has the medal but not the honest run, and she can be reported | You're right, Daisy should have cut it too | Medals are all that matter in sport | Corners don't count in cross-country
test: A cartoon shows an ancient Greek cheat getting a golden statue of himself as a reward. What is wrong? | Cheats paid for statues of Zeus with their names on as a shame | Nothing; cheats got statues as prizes | The statues were of the winners, not cheats | The Greeks didn't have any statues
test: Ellie's aunt says, "It's just a fake address, nobody gets hurt." What is the best reply? | Another real child loses the school place | She's right, it's a victimless trick that everyone does | Schools always have spare places for extra children | Only the postman gets confused about the letters
test: "Bro, just vote for me ten times, it's only a poll, don't be a killjoy," says Josh's friend. What should Josh do? | Vote once; one person, one vote | Vote ten times, because it's only a poll | Vote twenty times to make sure | Vote for himself instead
test: Kai says, "I got the skin by skill, swear." Later he admits he bought it. What did he lose? | Trust and real pride; pride in fake effort is empty | Nothing; buying skins is just as cool as unlocking them | Only his birthday money | His account, because buying is banned
test: What would happen if everyone could pay someone else to write their schoolwork? | Marks would mean nothing, and nobody would learn | Everyone would become cleverer, because they'd read more essays | Teachers would have more free time | Nothing would change at all
test: Freya's friend whispers, "If Miss asks, say I wasn't looking." What should Freya do? | Talk to her friend, and refuse to lie for him if asked | Say he wasn't looking, because being loyal always comes first | Say she didn't see anything at all | Tell Miss he cheats every day
test: "Y'all, this cheat sheet's gonna save me," says Brayden, who made it carefully for hours. What is funny about it? | Making it taught him everything on it | It was far too small for anyone to read | He forgot to bring it to school on the day | The teacher had made exactly the same sheet
test: Ali's friend says, "Only Christians think cheating is wrong." What is accurate? | Many faiths, including Islam and Judaism, teach that cheating is wrong | Only Christians have any rules about cheating | Muslims are allowed to cheat at markets | No religion says anything about cheating
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the custom at ancient Olympia whereby fines levied upon cheats were applied? | The erection of bronze statues of Zeus inscribed with the names of the offenders | The award of additional prizes to the victorious athletes | The construction of private residences for the judges | The return of the fines to the offenders at the conclusion of the Games
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the use of a false address to obtain a school place is not a victimless act. | Another child is thereby deprived of a place to which that child was entitled | The school is thereby obliged to construct additional classrooms at public expense | The postal service is thereby inconvenienced | No reason exists, the act being wholly victimless
exam: Select the statement consistent with the saying attributed to the Prophet Muhammad in Sahih Muslim. | Whoever cheats is not one of us | Whoever cheats shall be rewarded in the market | Whoever sells grain shall be suspected of cheating | Whoever cheats in small matters is excused
exam: Given that a pupil observes a friend cheating in a minor test, ascertain the course of action most consistent with this lesson. | The pupil ought first to speak to the friend and must not lie on the friend's behalf | The pupil ought to denounce the friend publicly forthwith | The pupil ought to adopt the friend's answers | The pupil ought to provide a false account if questioned
